A||||17||BlackStang95||Fiero seats ||||8||
Z||000000||BlackStang95||10-20-2001||07:14 PM||||Me and my friend (spectrum) decided to take out his fieros seats to clean under and around them, it was very dirty under.  It was very difficult to get to all the bolts, and coupled with the fact they were 13 mm bolts (metric bolts on a domestic?!) we had quite a time.  The worst part no doubt was putting the passenger seat back in...it lines up really funny and it took about a soild half hour of me picking it up and moving it to get it right. Whew!  Never doing that again!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Phil||10-20-2001||07:18 PM||||I think you will find that every nut and bolt  on a Fiero is metric.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_1.gif">     <BR>SC 3800||||reg||1
Z||000002||LT1 Chop Top||10-20-2001||07:19 PM||||I found it easier to scoot the seat foward when accessing rear bolts, and easier to access front bolts by scooting the seat back.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Monkeyman||10-20-2001||07:57 PM||||You need 3 sockets to work on a Fiero.  7mm, 10mm, and 13mm (and occasionally a 15mm).  Those and a T15 Torx and you've got yourself a roadside tool kit.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||SKIDMARK||10-20-2001||08:00 PM||||I had the seats (both) out, cleaned and bolted back in in about an hour. His must have really been rusty or cross threaded or something to take that long.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||10-20-2001||08:56 PM||||As long as you don't move the seat slide mechanisim when the seats are out, they should relign with no problem.  I find that if the seats get moved, each side wants to move independenly, then no more alignent and then lots of adjustments.  Other wise its a 10 minute job.  Plus you find lots of spare change under the seats.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||10-20-2001||11:16 PM||||Pretty much every GM car since the 80s will be near 100% metric.  All the measurements between bolts, frame rails, and everything that will have tolerances when they build the car is also in metric.<P>What I don't understand is why people get so pissed off when they find a metric bolt.  Geez, if you're going to work on cars, you'd better have a complete set of tools anyways, which includes metrics.  Be thankful GM didnt go completely propreitary when building the Fiero and make all the nuts and bolts Whitworth sizes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||10-20-2001||11:51 PM||||Like the 15.5 mm flare nut that hold the fuel line to the fuel pump on the carb equipped HO Camaros?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jake_Dragon||10-21-2001||12:02 AM||||The thing to watch for is not to move the rails till you have the seat in the car, they move independently of each other.||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||10-21-2001||12:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>I think you will find that every nut and bolt  on a Fiero is metric.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think thats true for all american cars these days.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Spektrum-87GT||10-21-2001||12:57 AM||||it was harder for us because we had to use a big old cresent wrench that barely fit anywhere. also, that passanger seat was near impossible to get back in! just wouldnt line up right. oh yeah... i've come to the conclusion that gm engineers suck. I've worked on chryslers and they're easy to work on and everything make sense. I've worked on a few gm cars and NOTHING ever makes sense. i occasionally find random pieces of metal that serve no purpose other than to get in the way(the stupid radio blocker comes to mind) Another thing that comes to mind is the backup light switch. its recessed in the transaxle... and uses a HUGE socket. nearly impossible to break, hard to fit the wrench in there in the first place. this is what im talking about.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Galen||10-21-2001||02:11 AM||||competence is the word, gotta have that. You have troubles removing and reinstalling seats ... and complain about a bolt being metric.. then that word isn't something you have..<P>------------------<BR><B>Ray</B> Webmaster of <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>Fierolinks</A><BR>"The price of love is tragedy"||||reg||1
Z||000012||Brandon86SE||10-21-2001||02:23 AM||||Metric rules!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Not only do I find the Fiero very easy to work with, only needing the 7mm, 15mm, and torx so far (previously mentioned by Monkeyman), but I also get to top out at 210, while you Americans only get to do 125  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000013||BlackStang95||10-21-2001||03:20 AM||||We did move the track with the seats out...i didnt think that would matter, we were trying to clean the nasty rusty things up.  Thanks for the advice though.  It doesn't matter that the socket was metric, it just surprised me...and we were doing this with a VERY limited set of tools cuz spek wouldnt drive over to my house (we didnt even have any sockets).  I think we did damn good for 2 guys with a single monkey wrench.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||10-21-2001||10:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>Be thankful GM didnt go completely propreitary when building the Fiero and make all the nuts and bolts Whitworth sizes.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Whitworth &lt;shudder&gt;<P>Just brought back memories of my old Triumph Bonneville.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fformula88||10-21-2001||10:34 AM||||Chryslers are engineered to be easy to work on because they must always be worked on to keep on the road!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Every Chrysler owned by someone I know has been a major mechanical headache!<P>I think having a more complete set of tools would have made your project a lot easier!  Its not GM's engineers fault that you didn't have access to anything other than one monkey wrench.||||reg||1
Z||000016||tomcat06085||10-21-2001||12:40 PM||||At least you did it instead of paying someone. Maybe found some change on the floor too!!! I had MG's and Mini Coopers back in the 1960's and woke up real fast when a tune-up went from $25.00 to $35.00. Being twenty years old, in college and married, I got some good old Craftsman socket sets and combination ratchet wrenches real quick. You'd think the English cars would have had metric engineering, but the MG's were about 99% American sizes....there was a bolt on the slave cylinder for the hydraulic clutch that was metric, but was easy to reach with a crescent wrench. Now, it costs $35.00 for oil, lube, wheel rotation. Then again, mechanics were making 3.00 bucks an hour. We don't need the good old days, just the elbow grease.<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Thanks for the help!||||reg||12
Z||000017||SLYGUY||10-21-2001||02:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>You need 3 sockets to work on a Fiero.  7mm, 10mm, and 13mm (and occasionally a 15mm).  Those and a T15 Torx and you've got yourself a roadside tool kit.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree with you on all of the metric size fastners on this car. I haven't used domestic sizes yet. <BR>Oh, if you include a 14mm you've got it complete. <BR>The 14mm is for the connections to the clutch master cylinder  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
